<header>
<h1 class="p-name">WHAT HAPPENED TO ANDROID TABLETS?</h1>
</header>
<section data-field="subtitle" class="p-summary">
Around 5 to 6 years ago, almost every top brand had a tablet series beside their smartphone line up. But today hardly a couple of brands…
</section>
<section data-field="body" class="e-content">
<section name="b17e" class="section section--body section--first section--last"><div class="section-divider"><hr class="section-divider"></div><div class="section-content"><div class="section-inner sectionLayout--insetColumn"><h3 name="83db" id="83db" class="graf graf--h3 graf--leading graf--title"><strong class="markup--strong markup--h3-strong">WHAT HAPPENED TO ANDROID TABLETS?</strong></h3><figure name="35ec" id="35ec" class="graf graf--figure graf-after--h3"><img class="graf-image" data-image-id="0*m3Oc5mybBhrDfJFQ.jpg" data-width="320" data-height="213" data-is-featured="true" src="https://cdn-images-1.medium.com/max/800/0*m3Oc5mybBhrDfJFQ.jpg"></figure><p name="cc08" id="cc08" class="graf graf--p graf-after--figure">Around 5 to 6 years ago, almost every top brand had a tablet series beside their smartphone line up. But today hardly a couple of brands still retain their tablet line up in the Android segment. Whereas in the iOS segment, Apple constantly update their iPad line up. Compared to Android, the iPad has always been a successful line up for Apple. Every year, along with their iPhone they keep launching a new version of iPad.</p><p name="64e6" id="64e6" class="graf graf--p graf-after--p"><strong class="markup--strong markup--p-strong">Android tablets remain unnoticed. What is the reason?</strong></p><figure name="c9eb" id="c9eb" class="graf graf--figure graf-after--p"><img class="graf-image" data-image-id="0*QdfhGsb3PZXR-6pM.jpg" data-width="320" data-height="213" src="https://cdn-images-1.medium.com/max/800/0*QdfhGsb3PZXR-6pM.jpg"></figure><p name="5163" id="5163" class="graf graf--p graf-after--figure">When Apple released their first iPad in <strong class="markup--strong markup--p-strong">2010, </strong>It was received positively by everyone. Everyone liked its form factor and design. This led to the release of the first Android tablet,<strong class="markup--strong markup--p-strong"> the Galaxy tab</strong>. Even before that, there were many Chinese slates that were so-called tablets. This was the first non-chinese tab. The build and design was good for a seven-inch tablet. But the software optimization was terrible.</p><p name="8aa7" id="8aa7" class="graf graf--p graf-after--p"><strong class="markup--strong markup--p-strong">Android Gingerbread</strong> was designed only for smartphones. It was not designed for a tablet experience. Widgets were not re-sizable in those days.</p><p name="f710" id="f710" class="graf graf--p graf-after--p">So Google took the initiative to launch<strong class="markup--strong markup--p-strong"> android Honeycomb</strong>. An android version designed specifically for tablets. It had features like re sizable widgets and it was better than the previous version. At this time, brands like Amazon, Motorola, started making their own tablets.</p><figure name="450d" id="450d" class="graf graf--figure graf-after--p"><img class="graf-image" data-image-id="0*coSgeaQCn3yi5uyk.jpg" data-width="213" data-height="320" src="https://cdn-images-1.medium.com/max/800/0*coSgeaQCn3yi5uyk.jpg"></figure><p name="0e65" id="0e65" class="graf graf--p graf-after--figure">But the problem was, they were overpriced. Eventhough the Amazon fire tablet was selling for half the price of Galaxy Tab, it was more like an e-reader. Motorola Xoom retailed for $500. With the release of the next version of android ice cream sandwich, Google released their own tablet.</p><p name="dff4" id="dff4" class="graf graf--p graf-after--p"><strong class="markup--strong markup--p-strong">The Nexus 7</strong> was a 7 inch tablet. Since it had a 7 inch display compared to the 10 inch tablets at that time, apps did not seem to be comically disproportionate. It was a bit stretched but usable and the pricing was fine.</p><p name="5048" id="5048" class="graf graf--p graf-after--p">It was released for $200 which was great and it did not have any bloatware unlike the Chinese tablets. Ice cream sandwich was designed for both tablets and smartphones. But after honeycomb, no other version of android was truly optimized for tablet experience.</p><p name="4b9a" id="4b9a" class="graf graf--p graf-after--p">Even though android is capable of adjusting the app size to the screen size, generally it would have white bars on the sides that looked awful.</p><p name="9dd5" id="9dd5" class="graf graf--p graf-after--p"><strong class="markup--strong markup--p-strong">What Apple did right ?</strong></p><figure name="33c3" id="33c3" class="graf graf--figure graf-after--p"><img class="graf-image" data-image-id="0*EzemAupANo5rnLn0.jpg" data-width="320" data-height="213" src="https://cdn-images-1.medium.com/max/800/0*EzemAupANo5rnLn0.jpg"></figure><p name="7831" id="7831" class="graf graf--p graf-after--figure">The reason why iPad was successful is because it received constant updates from Apple. iPads always came with the current generation <strong class="markup--strong markup--p-strong">flagship processor</strong> as in the iPhone while Android tablets were running in midrange processor. Developers took advantage of this lucrative hardware and developed apps specifically for the iPad. The biggest reason is, iOS was exceptionally optimized for the iPad experience.</p><p name="f897" id="f897" class="graf graf--p graf-after--p"><strong class="markup--strong markup--p-strong">What happened to Android tablets?</strong></p><p name="dc06" id="dc06" class="graf graf--p graf-after--p">When the tablets were first introduced, the maximum screen size of a smartphone was below 4 inches. This made tablets stand apart. But as technology evolved, the screen size of smartphones also evolved. It kept on increasing, thereby reducing the difference between a smartphone and a tablet. Now as the screen size of the smartphone is bigger, the need for a tablet felt unnecessary.</p><figure name="e908" id="e908" class="graf graf--figure graf-after--p"><img class="graf-image" data-image-id="0*yJAJXmiTnpGJMYMY.jpg" data-width="320" data-height="213" src="https://cdn-images-1.medium.com/max/800/0*yJAJXmiTnpGJMYMY.jpg"></figure><p name="250a" id="250a" class="graf graf--p graf-after--figure">So, people stopped buying tablets and so brands stopped making tablets. As of 2020, the iPad still remains a successful line up for Apple. Only Samsung and a few other brands retained their tablet line up and still release tablets time to time.</p><p name="0d92" id="0d92" class="graf graf--p graf-after--p"><strong class="markup--strong markup--p-strong">Fun fact:</strong></p><p name="0db7" id="0db7" class="graf graf--p graf-after--p">Even I had a tablet back in 2014. It was from AOC and it had 1 GB RAM and 2 GB of internal storage, which was more than enough. I used it for nearly 3 years and I liked it very much. It was my first tablet. <strong class="markup--strong markup--p-strong">Let me know in the comments section, what was your first tablet?</strong></p><p name="bcdb" id="bcdb" class="graf graf--p graf-after--p graf--trailing"><strong class="markup--strong markup--p-strong">P.S</strong>: If you share this, it would make my day.</p></div></div></section>
</section>
<footer><p>By <a href="https://medium.com/@gokulrece23" class="p-author h-card">Gokul Rajan</a> on <a href="https://medium.com/p/dbc83fbe1b91"><time class="dt-published" datetime="2025-04-12T03:40:58.323Z">April 12, 2025</time></a>.</p><p><a href="https://medium.com/@gokulrece23/what-happened-to-android-tablets-dbc83fbe1b91" class="p-canonical">Canonical link</a></p><p>Exported from <a href="https://medium.com">Medium</a> on April 28, 2026.</p></footer></article></body></html>